ck later on in Bergen Belsen  I realized that my sister was being  watched by the camp doctor they I do not  know who it was he was a German doctor  but he would come by the children's  barracks all the time come to my sister  pinch  tree Explorer years and try to be very  friendly with her and then one day he  said to me that he would be able to give  us all interesting chocolates if I  allowed my sister to go into the  hospital for a few days and my sister  was perfectly well so I couldn't  understand why you wanted her in the  hospital and I just was very sassy and I  said no you are not because if you are  I'm going to kick you and I went up and  sort of said I am really going to I'm  serious I mean it and so he laughed and  then forgot about it later on the block  has to told me that he had hoped to be  able to use my sister for scientific  research right across from us with a  channel house filled with corpses not  just the inside but overflowing oil that  they were corpses all over  I lived walked beside that people and  after a while it just got to be so that  one noticed and one had to say to  oneself I'm not going to see who with  this  I'm not going to recognize anyone in  this person who is lying there and it  got to the point where I realized that I  had to close my eyes to a number of  things and otherwise I would not have  survived even at that time because I saw  people around me going mad I was not  only having to live with all these  things but with madness  one thing I did receive from the woman  inside was a small bag of tea which I  was able during the time of the typhus  epidemic to exchange for a vaccine for  my sister  and I was told it was a typhus vaccine  whether it was won or not I'm not sure  but the fact is my sister never did get  the typist I did and so what one of the  saddest thing in my life has been that I  have no recollection of the liberation  because I was totally Arabic the Tigris  I have no recollection of what happened  when the English came to bergen-belsen  I was very near death had I had to wait  another two days for the English I would  not have survived and the it's I have  proved it on that that I have not been  conscious at what I consider to be the  most important experience I came to  Sweden I was given over to a doctor in  Sweden who immediately decided to fatten  me up because I weighed at the age of 11  I weighed as much as a three-year-old  child and had a big fat belly  my sister is alive she came to New York  with me she went to a school she went to  the Lexington School for the Deaf she  married she has three children coming to  the United States was a great shock I  happen to come on in August of 1948 it's  the hottest day in the history of the  United States I had come from Sweden I  walked out of the plane and I remember  turning around and saying I'm going back  because not only was it very very hot  but at the airport greeting this plane  which had taken us 24 hours to fly from  Stockholm to New York I was suddenly  faced by a whole panoply of black faces  nobody in Sweden had told me but there  were Negroes in the United States I knew  that they were in Africa but suddenly  come into New York and see all those  black faces I was sure I had come into  the wrong country I was supposed to come  to the United States he was this hot  place with all the black people I  thought that was some way in Africa I  had landed somewhere in Africa it was so  hot that's right it was exactly it was  it was my having in some way sense that  life is playing tricks on me all the  time that I wasn't going to the places I  thought I was going to and then at the  ramp where my relatives waiting for me  none of whom I knew I had never met them  they had all left long before I was born  they were yelling and screaming grenade  and Aron a so I knew that I was where I  was supposed to be but what was strange  was having to get adjusted not only to a  totally different mirror but to  relatives are got absolutely no inkling  of  you
 

